var OPHEP_S14_DRAFT = [

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-1", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Consecutive optic atrophy occurs in:", options:["Optic neuritis","Papilledema","Glaucoma","Retinitis pigmentosa"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Consecutive optic atrophy is the type that follows disease of the retina, and retinitis pigmentosa is a degenerative retinal disease that ends in loss of the ganglion cell axons forming the optic nerve (not taken from the course material). Four types of optic atrophy are recognised: primary, secondary, consecutive and glaucomatous (L21, slide 14). The consecutive type shows a waxy yellow disc with markedly thinned, thread-like arterioles against a washed-out retina (L21, slide 14). A further fundus shows a waxy pale disc, attenuated vessels and coarse black pigment clumps over the mid-periphery of the retina (L21, slide 16). That picture is retinitis pigmentosa (not taken from the course material).\n\nOptic neuritis is wrong because it starts in the optic nerve, not the retina. It is unilateral, with reduced vision, reduced colour vision, a central scotoma and a relative afferent pupillary defect (L21, slide 8). Atrophy after it is classed as primary or secondary, not consecutive (not taken from the course material).\n\nPapilledema is wrong because it is bilateral swelling of the disc from raised intracranial pressure, with normal acuity and colour vision (L21, slide 8). The secondary pattern is a dirty grey-white disc with blurred, indistinct margins (L21, slide 14). Atrophy that follows disc swelling takes this pattern, not the consecutive one (not taken from the course material).\n\nGlaucoma is wrong because it has its own type, glaucomatous atrophy, in which the pallor is confined to a deep, large cup with a sharp margin rather than spread across the whole disc (L21, slide 14).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Consecutive optic atrophy follows degenerative retinal diseases like retinitis pigmentosa, where retinal degeneration leads to secondary optic nerve atrophy. It differs from primary optic atrophy, which originates from optic nerve pathology.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise consecutive optic atrophy as the atrophy that follows retinal disease such as retinitis pigmentosa, and distinguish it from primary, secondary and glaucomatous optic atrophy.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1014" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-2", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A pituitary adenoma produces:", options:["Binasal hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","A central scotoma","An arcuate scotoma"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"A pituitary adenoma sits beneath the optic chiasm, and the chiasm is where the nasal retinal fibres of the two eyes cross. The pituitary lies directly under the middle of the chiasm, and the field defect from a chiasmal lesion is bitemporal hemianopia, the outer half of each eye's field lost with the nasal halves spared (L22, slide 15). The nasal and temporal retinal fibres are drawn in separate colours so that the crossing at the chiasm is visible (L22, slide 15). A midline mass presses on the crossing nasal fibres, which carry each eye's temporal field, so both temporal fields go (not taken from the course material). Pituitary adenoma is the classic mass in this position (not taken from the course material).\n\nBinasal hemianopia is wrong because it is drawn as the field loss of masses pressing on the chiasm from both lateral sides (L22, slide 16). A single adenoma growing up from the midline does not press on the chiasm that way (not taken from the course material).\n\nA central scotoma is wrong because it is the field defect of acute optic neuritis, a disease of the optic nerve in front of the chiasm (L21, slide 4).\n\nAn arcuate scotoma is wrong because it is a glaucomatous field defect from damage at the optic nerve head, listed among the field changes of open angle glaucoma (L11).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> A pituitary adenoma compresses the optic chiasm, where nasal fibers from both eyes cross, leading to bitemporal hemianopia (loss of temporal visual fields in both eyes).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Predict bitemporal hemianopia from a mass compressing the midline of the optic chiasm, and distinguish it from binasal hemianopia, central scotoma and arcuate scotoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1016" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-3", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Unilateral dilated pupil is not caused by:", options:["Horner syndrome","Ocular trauma","Atropine toxicity","Third cranial nerve palsy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Horner syndrome is a sympathetic paralysis, and the sympathetic supply drives the dilator iridis through the long ciliary nerves (L22, slide 2). Losing it leaves the parasympathetic sphincter pupillae unopposed, so the pupil is small, not dilated (not taken from the course material). Miosis is listed as a neurologic effect of sympathetic paralysis (Horner's syndrome) (L22, slide 3), and its features are miosis, dilation lag, ptosis, apparent enophthalmos and anhidrosis (L22, slide 11). A unilateral dilated pupil is therefore not caused by Horner syndrome.\n\nOcular trauma is wrong because trauma is a listed ocular cause of mydriasis (L22, slide 4), so a dilated pupil after an injury is an expected outcome.\n\nAtropine toxicity is wrong because the parasympatholytic drugs are a listed pharmacologic cause of mydriasis (L22, slide 4). Atropine is a parasympatholytic (not taken from the course material). Blocking the parasympathetic supply to the sphincter leaves the pupil dilated (L22, slide 2).\n\nThird cranial nerve palsy is wrong because parasympathetic paralysis from a third nerve palsy is a listed neurologic cause of mydriasis (L22, slide 4). The parasympathetic fibres to the sphincter pupillae are lost, so the pupil dilates.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Horner syndrome causes miosis (small pupil), not dilation. Dilated pupils result from third nerve palsy, ocular trauma, or exposure to anticholinergic agents like atropine.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Separate the causes of a dilated pupil (third nerve palsy, trauma, parasympatholytic drugs) from Horner syndrome, which causes miosis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1018" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-4", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Afferent pupillary defect is seen in:", options:["Third nerve paralysis","Sympathetic paralysis","Optic atrophy","Lesions of the occipital cortex"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"A relative afferent pupillary defect is one of the two starred signs of optic nerve dysfunction, along with a field defect (L21, slide 3). The swinging flashlight test confirms it as the Marcus Gunn pupil (L31,32, slide 6). Light in the fellow eye constricts both pupils, but as the torch swings to the defective eye both pupils dilate again (L22, slide 6). Papillitis carries the defect strongly and papilledema does not (L21, slide 8). Optic atrophy is optic nerve damage, so it fits, and it is listed among the ocular conditions with a dilated pupil (L22, slide 4).\n\nThird nerve paralysis is wrong because it is a parasympathetic paralysis with a dilated pupil (L22, slide 4). It is an efferent fault, so the eye still registers light and no afferent defect appears (not taken from the course material).\n\nSympathetic paralysis is wrong because it is Horner syndrome, with miosis, dilation lag, ptosis, apparent enophthalmos and anhidrosis (L22, slides 3 and 11). None of these signs is afferent.\n\nLesions of the occipital cortex are wrong because they cause a contralateral homonymous hemianopia (L22, slide 23). The pupillary light reflex fibres leave the visual pathway before the cortex, so a cortical lesion leaves the light reflex intact (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> An afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil) occurs when the optic nerve is damaged, as in optic atrophy. The pupil constricts less when light is shone in the affected eye.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify a relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil) as a sign of optic nerve disease such as optic atrophy, and distinguish it from efferent, sympathetic and cortical lesions.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1020" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-5", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A left optic tract lesion is manifested by:", options:["Right homonymous hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Binasal hemianopia"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"An optic tract lesion causes a contralateral homonymous hemianopia, and the defect is incongruous (L22, slide 17). A right tract lesion gives loss of the left half of the field in both eyes (L22, slide 24), so by the same rule a left tract lesion gives loss of the right half of the field in both eyes, a right homonymous hemianopia. The reason is that the tract carries the crossed nasal fibres from the opposite eye and the uncrossed temporal fibres from the same eye, and both sets see the right half of the field (not taken from the course material).\n\nLeft homonymous hemianopia is wrong because it needs a lesion on the right, behind the chiasm: the right tract gives a left homonymous hemianopia (L22, slide 24).\n\nBitemporal hemianopia is wrong because it comes from a midline chiasmal lesion, with the outer half of each field lost (L22, slides 15 and 24). A tract lesion sits behind the chiasm and takes the same half of the field from both eyes, not the outer half of each (L22, slide 24).\n\nBinasal hemianopia is wrong because it is drawn as the loss from masses pressing on both sides of the chiasm, with the inner half of each field lost (L22, slide 16). A single tract lesion is one-sided and does not give this pair.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> A left optic tract lesion affects the right visual fields of both eyes, producing right homonymous hemianopia.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Predict the field defect of an optic tract lesion as a contralateral homonymous hemianopia, and separate it from chiasmal patterns.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1022" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-6", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Marcus Gunn pupil is found in which condition?", options:["Compressive optic neuropathy","Glaucoma","Cataract","Retinal detachment"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A Marcus Gunn pupil is a relative afferent pupillary defect. Light in the fellow eye constricts both pupils, but as the torch swings to the defective eye both pupils dilate again (L22, slide 6). The defect is one of the two starred signs of optic nerve dysfunction, along with a field defect (L21, slide 3). Compression of the optic nerve is an optic nerve lesion that causes sector or wedge field defects (L21, slide 4), so compressive optic neuropathy is the answer.\n\nGlaucoma is wrong because it damages the optic nerve head, but a relative defect appears only when the two nerves are damaged unequally, so in symmetrical disease the pupils react equally (not taken from the course material). It is not the typical setting for a Marcus Gunn pupil.\n\nCataract is wrong because it is an opacity of the lens in front of the retina and does not damage the afferent pathway. A relative afferent defect found in a patient with cataract signals another pathology (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it is a disease of the retina, and a defect appears only when a large area of retina has detached (not taken from the course material). A lesion of the optic nerve is the classic setting for a Marcus Gunn pupil.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> A Marcus Gunn pupil (relative afferent pupillary defect) occurs in optic nerve lesions, such as compressive optic neuropathy. The affected eye shows reduced constriction to direct light.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise the Marcus Gunn pupil (relative afferent pupillary defect) as a sign of optic nerve disease such as compressive optic neuropathy, and distinguish it from glaucoma, cataract and retinal detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1024" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-7", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 55-year-old patient presents with ptosis, diplopia, and the eye deviated “down and out.” The pupil is dilated and non-reactive to light. What is the most common cause of this presentation?", options:["Posterior communicating artery aneurysm","Diabetic neuropathy","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Multiple sclerosis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The picture is a complete third nerve palsy with the pupil involved: ptosis, an eye that sits down and out, and a dilated pupil (L30, slides 11 and 12). Third nerve paralysis is a parasympathetic paralysis that dilates the pupil (L22, slide 4). The pupil decides the cause. A third nerve palsy with the pupil involved is labelled compression, and one with the pupil spared is labelled vascular (L30, slide 13). The diagram on that slide shows a posterior communicating artery aneurysm pressing on the pupillary fibres and producing pupillary dilation, while vascular disease of the vasa vasorum infarcts the central fibres of the nerve and leaves the pupil normal (L30, slide 13). The pupillary fibres are drawn around the outer rim of the nerve, so an external mass reaches them first (L30, slide 13). This patient has a dilated, non-reactive pupil, so compression by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm fits. It is the classic and commonest compressive cause of a third nerve palsy (not taken from the course material). An aneurysm can rupture into a subarachnoid haemorrhage, so this is an emergency (not taken from the course material).\n\nDiabetic neuropathy is wrong because a vascular third nerve palsy spares the pupil (L30, slide 13). Diabetes is a microvascular cause of such a palsy (not taken from the course material).\n\nCavernous sinus thrombosis is wrong because it is uncommon and usually involves several ocular motor nerves together with proptosis and chemosis, not an isolated third nerve palsy (not taken from the course material). Involvement of the ocular motor nerves in the cavernous sinus is a listed cause of ophthalmoplegia (L10, slide 10).\n\nMultiple sclerosis is wrong because demyelination is an uncommon cause of an isolated third nerve palsy with a dilated pupil in a 55-year-old (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Aneurysm compressing the oculomotor nerve causes ptosis, “down and out” eye, and pupil dilation. This is an emergency due to risk of rupture and subarachnoid hemorrhage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise a pupil-involving complete third nerve palsy, and link the pupil-involved pattern to compression by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm rather than to vascular, demyelinating or cavernous sinus causes.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1026" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-8", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 60-year-old man presents with ptosis, miosis, and enophthalmos. Which investigation should be done to identify the cause?", options:["Echocardiography","MRI thorax","MRI abdomen"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Ptosis, miosis and enophthalmos are the signs of Horner syndrome: miosis, dilation lag, ptosis, apparent enophthalmos and anhidrosis (L22, slide 11). It arises from a lesion anywhere on the sympathetic pathway, a three-neurone chain. The route runs from the hypothalamus down the cord to T1, out to the superior cervical ganglion, up along the carotid artery, and through the cavernous sinus to the dilator pupillae, Muller's muscle and the other orbital structures (L22, slide 10). The segment leaving the cord at T1 passes over the apex of the lung (not taken from the course material). An apical lung (Pancoast) tumour is therefore the classic cause of Horner syndrome in a 60-year-old man, and imaging the chest is the investigation that finds it (not taken from the course material). MRI thorax is the answer.\n\nEchocardiography is wrong because the heart is not a station on the sympathetic route (L22, slide 10), so it cannot show the cause of the lesion (not taken from the course material).\n\nMRI abdomen is wrong because the pathway leaves the cord at T1 (L22, slide 10), in the upper chest, and an abdominal image lies below the level of the lesion (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> This presentation suggests Horner syndrome, which may result from a lesion along the sympathetic pathway commonly due to apical lung (Pancoast) tumor. Therefore, MRI of the thorax is the best investigation to identify the underlying cause.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise Horner syndrome, trace the sympathetic pathway from the hypothalamus through T1 to the eye, and choose thoracic imaging to look for an apical lung tumour.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1028" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-9", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 45-year-old man presents with unilateral ptosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos, and anisocoria that is more apparent in dim lighting. What is the most likely diagnosis?", options:["Oculomotor nerve palsy","Horner syndrome","Myasthenia gravis","Adie’s pupil"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The findings are ptosis, miosis, dilation lag, anhidrosis and apparent enophthalmos, which together make Horner syndrome (L22, slide 11). It is a sympathetic paralysis, so the dilator iridis is lost and the pupil is small (L22, slides 2 and 3). The anisocoria is more apparent in dim light because the normal pupil dilates in the dark (L22, slide 4), while the Horner pupil is slow to dilate. That lag is one of the listed signs (L22, slide 11). This is why an anisocoria should be looked at in both light and dark (L22, slide 12). The enophthalmos is only apparent (L22, slide 11), an effect of the narrowed palpebral fissure (not taken from the course material).\n\nOculomotor nerve palsy is wrong because it is a parasympathetic paralysis with a dilated pupil (L22, slide 4), and the eye sits down and out (L30, slide 11). It does not cause anhidrosis or apparent enophthalmos, and the anisocoria is greater in bright light because the abnormal pupil is the large one that cannot constrict (not taken from the course material).\n\nMyasthenia gravis is wrong because it causes fatigable ptosis and diplopia with normal pupils, so it gives no anisocoria and no anhidrosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nAdie's pupil is wrong because it is a large, tonic pupil that reacts poorly to light and slowly to near, with no ptosis and no anhidrosis (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Horner syndrome is characterized by ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis,\n>\n> and enophthalmos. The anisocoria is more evident in dim light\n>\n> because the affected pupil fails to dilate.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Diagnose Horner syndrome from ptosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos and anisocoria that is greater in the dark, and distinguish it from oculomotor palsy, myasthenia gravis and Adie's pupil.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1030" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-10", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"An elderly patient presents with sudden visual loss, headache, jaw claudication, and scalp tenderness. Which investigation is most important to confirm the diagnosis?", options:["ESR","CT brain","MRI orbit","Visual field testing"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Sudden visual loss with headache, jaw claudication and scalp tenderness in an elderly patient is giant cell arteritis (temporal arteritis) until proved otherwise (not taken from the course material). The visual loss is anterior ischemic optic neuropathy caused by the arteritis (not taken from the course material). The AION slide shows a thickened, tortuous, beaded temporal artery standing out from the skin of an elderly patient, a swollen pale disc, and a field defect that cuts off along the horizontal meridian (L21, slide 9). A 90-year-old woman with vision loss in one eye, persistent headache, fatigue and weight loss had anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (L31,32, slides 20 and 23).\n\nESR is the answer because it is the immediate blood test in this picture. It is raised in giant cell arteritis and is checked together with CRP before steroid treatment is started (not taken from the course material). Temporal artery biopsy is the later histological step (not taken from the course material).\n\nCT brain is wrong because the disease is an inflammation of the arteries, not a lesion inside the brain, so the scan does not show it (not taken from the course material).\n\nMRI orbit is wrong because it looks for orbital disease and does not detect the systemic inflammation of giant cell arteritis (not taken from the course material).\n\nVisual field testing is wrong because it records the field loss, an altitudinal defect in anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (L21, slide 4), but it does not identify the arteritis behind it.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Giant cell arteritis (temporal arteritis) presents with vision loss, jaw claudication, and scalp tenderness. ESR (and CRP) are crucial for diagnosis and should be checked immediately.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise giant cell arteritis from sudden visual loss, headache, jaw claudication and scalp tenderness in an elderly patient, and choose ESR (with CRP) as the first investigation.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1032" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-11", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 50-year-old patient presents with severe headache, nausea, and projectile vomiting. On ophthalmologic examination, bilateral blurred disc margins are noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?", options:["Optic neuritis","Papilledema","Retinal detachment","Central retinal vein occlusion"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Bilateral blurred disc margins with severe headache, nausea and projectile vomiting are papilledema, disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure. Headache, nausea and vomiting are the general symptoms of raised intracranial pressure (not taken from the course material). Papilledema has raised intracranial pressure as its etiology and is bilateral, with normal visual acuity, colour vision and contrast, an enlarged blind spot, no relative afferent pupillary defect, a clear vitreous and marked disc elevation (L21, slide 8). The disc is hyperaemic with obliterated margins, engorged tortuous vessels and flame haemorrhages, and both eyes are involved (L21, slides 5 and 6).\n\nOptic neuritis is wrong because it is unilateral, with reduced vision, colour vision and contrast, a central scotoma and a relative afferent pupillary defect, and only mild disc elevation (L21, slide 8). Its causes lie in the nerve itself (multiple sclerosis, viral, toxic), not in raised intracranial pressure (L21, slide 8).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes and a curtain-like loss of field in one eye (L31,32, slide 26), not with headache, vomiting and bilateral disc swelling.\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because it is usually unilateral, with sudden painless loss of vision and a swollen disc with dilated, tortuous veins and widespread retinal haemorrhages, and it does not cause headache and projectile vomiting (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Bilateral blurred disc margins with symptoms of raised intracranial pressure indicate papilledema. It results from increased intracranial pressure causing optic disc swelling.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Diagnose papilledema from bilateral blurred disc margins with symptoms of raised intracranial pressure, and distinguish it from optic neuritis, retinal detachment and central retinal vein occlusion.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1034" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-12", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Most common cause of bilateral abducent nerve palsy?", options:["Elevated intracranial pressure","Intracranial tumor","Trauma","Infection"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A sixth nerve palsy shows as esotropia in the primary position, with full adduction and deficient abduction (L30, slide 15). Raised intracranial pressure is the most common cause of a bilateral palsy (not taken from the course material). The abducent nerve has a long intracranial course from the pons, up the clivus and over the petrous ridge, and raised pressure stretches it and tethers it there on both sides at once (not taken from the course material). The palsy is a false localizing sign, because the nerve is damaged although the lesion is not on it (not taken from the course material). Raised intracranial pressure also causes papilledema (L21, slide 8), so disc swelling is expected to accompany the palsy.\n\nIntracranial tumour is wrong because a tumour damages the nerve either by direct compression, which tends to affect one nerve, or by raising the intracranial pressure, which is the more usual route to a bilateral palsy (not taken from the course material).\n\nTrauma is wrong because it can damage the nerve, but it is a less common cause of bilateral involvement (not taken from the course material).\n\nInfection is wrong because meningitis and other intracranial infections can involve the nerve, but they are less common than raised intracranial pressure as a cause of bilateral palsy (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> ↑ Intracranial pressure causes downward traction on both abducens nerves as they run along the clivus, leading to bilateral abducent nerve palsy. It's the most common cause due to the long intracranial course of CN VI.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise that raised intracranial pressure is the most common cause of bilateral abducent nerve palsy, and know the sixth nerve palsy pattern of esotropia with limited abduction.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1036" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-13", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Cause of pinpoint pupil?", options:["Organophosphorus poisoning","Mydriatics","Glaucoma","Horner syndrome"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A pinpoint pupil is extreme miosis. Pesticides and morphine are listed among the systemic pharmacologic causes of miosis, together with local parasympathomimetics (L22, slide 3). Organophosphorus compounds are pesticides. They inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine accumulates at the parasympathetic endings (not taken from the course material). The sphincter pupillae is supplied by acetylcholine through the short ciliary nerves (L22, slide 2), so the excess acetylcholine drives the sphincter and the pupil constricts to a pinpoint.\n\nMydriatics are wrong because they dilate the pupil. Parasympatholytics and sympathomimetics are listed pharmacologic causes of mydriasis (L22, slide 4).\n\nGlaucoma is wrong because acute congestive glaucoma is a listed cause of a dilated pupil (L22, slide 4). In acute angle closure the pupil is mid-dilated, vertically oval and non-reactive.\n\nHorner syndrome is wrong even though it causes miosis (L22, slides 3 and 11). Its miosis is one-sided and mild, with a dilation lag, and is not the extreme bilateral constriction of a pinpoint pupil (not taken from the course material). The printed box lists Horner syndrome among the causes of a dilated pupil, but L22 (slides 3 and 11) lists it as a cause of miosis; the key stands.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Organophosphorus compounds inhibit acetylcholinesterase, leading to excess acetylcholine and constricted (pinpoint) pupils. Mydriatics and Horner syndrome cause dilated pupils instead.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify organophosphorus poisoning as a cause of pinpoint pupils, and distinguish it from mydriatic drugs, glaucoma and the milder miosis of Horner syndrome.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1038" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-14", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 60-year-old man reports sudden, transient, painless loss of vision in one eye lasting a few minutes, which resolved spontaneously. What is the most likely diagnosis?", options:["Amaurosis fugax","Migraine without aura","Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Sudden, painless loss of vision in one eye that lasts a few minutes and then resolves is amaurosis fugax. It is transient monocular loss from ischemia of the retina, often from emboli from the carotid artery (not taken from the course material). The loss clears within minutes because the embolus passes, so nothing persists on examination (not taken from the course material). A 60-year-old man is in the age group for atherosclerotic carotid disease (not taken from the course material).\n\nMigraine without aura is wrong because it is a headache disorder with no visual aura, and the visual disturbance of migraine is usually bilateral and accompanied by headache (not taken from the course material).\n\nOptic neuritis is wrong because the loss persists. It gives unilateral loss of vision with a central scotoma and a relative afferent pupillary defect (L21, slide 8), as in the 24-year-old woman with sudden loss of vision in her left eye, acuity of 20/100, a relative afferent pupillary defect and a swollen disc, whose diagnosis was papillitis (L31,32, slides 12 to 16). Optic neuritis does not resolve within minutes (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it gives floating black dots, flashing lights and a curtain-like field loss over days (L31,32, slide 26), not a painless blackout that resolves within minutes (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Amaurosis fugax is transient monocular vision loss caused by retinal ischemia, often from carotid artery emboli. Vision typically returns within minutes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise amaurosis fugax as transient painless monocular visual loss from retinal ischemia, and separate it from migraine, optic neuritis and retinal detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1040" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-15", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 65-year-old patient presents with sudden, painless, and persistent loss of vision in one eye. Fundus examination shows a pale retina with a swollen optic disc. What is the most likely diagnosis?", options:["Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Central retinal vein occlusion","Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Sudden, painless, persistent loss of vision in one eye with a swollen optic disc in a 65-year-old is anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, caused by a sudden interruption of the blood supply to the optic nerve head (not taken from the course material). The disc is swollen but pale, with vessels of normal calibre, unlike the hyperaemic disc with engorged vessels of papilledema (L21, slide 5). The field defect is altitudinal, respecting the horizontal meridian (L21, slide 9), and the swollen pale disc later becomes a pale flat disc (L21, slide 9). A 90-year-old hypertensive woman with vision loss in her right eye, acuity of 20/80, a right relative afferent pupillary defect, inferior field loss and a swollen disc with flame-shaped haemorrhages had this diagnosis (L31,32, slides 20 to 23).\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because the disc is hyperaemic and swollen with dilated, tortuous veins and widespread haemorrhages, and the retina is not pale (not taken from the course material).\n\nOptic neuritis is wrong because it gives a central scotoma rather than an altitudinal defect (L21, slide 8), and it typically affects younger patients (not taken from the course material), as in the 24-year-old woman with papillitis (L31,32, slides 12 to 16).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floating black dots, flashing lights and a curtain-like loss of field (L31,32, slide 26), and the optic disc is not swollen in it (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> This condition results from sudden interruption of blood supply to the optic nerve head, leading to a pale swollen disc and permanent vision loss. It is common in elderly patients with vascular risk factors.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise anterior ischemic optic neuropathy from sudden painless persistent monocular visual loss with a pale swollen disc in an elderly patient, and separate it from central retinal vein occlusion, optic neuritis and retinal detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1042" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-16", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Which of the following is a condition characterized by tertiary syphilis with midbrain affection?", options:["Horner syndrome","Argyll-Robertson syndrome","Oculomotor palsy","Dorsal midbrain syndrome"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Argyll-Robertson pupils are the pupil sign of tertiary syphilis with midbrain involvement. The Argyll Robertson pupil is an affection of the pretectal area with small pupils and light-near dissociation, caused by neurosyphilis and also seen in diabetes mellitus, alcoholism and encephalitis (L22, slide 8). The photograph shows both pupils small and irregular (L22, slide 8). In light-near dissociation the pupil constricts poorly to light but constricts normally to a near target (not taken from the course material). Tertiary syphilis is the late stage of the infection, the stage that includes neurosyphilis (not taken from the course material).\n\nDorsal midbrain syndrome is wrong because, although it also shows light-near dissociation, it is caused by pineal body tumors, stroke or multiple sclerosis and comes with upward gaze paralysis, convergence-retraction nystagmus and lid retraction (L22, slide 9). Neurosyphilis is not listed among its causes (L22, slide 9).\n\nHorner syndrome is wrong because it is a lesion of the sympathetic pathway, which runs from the hypothalamus to T1, the superior cervical ganglion and the carotid artery (L22, slide 10). It gives miosis, dilation lag, ptosis, apparent enophthalmos and anhydrosis (L22, slide 11). The pupil still reacts to light (not taken from the course material).\n\nOculomotor palsy is wrong because a third nerve lesion gives a dilated pupil (L22, slide 4), not small pupils. It also gives ptosis and an eye turned down and out (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Argyll-Robertson pupils → Tertiary syphilis with midbrain affection. It is bilateral (may be asymmetric). It is characterized by small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation. Investigations: serologic testing for syphilis and MRI of the brainstem.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Link Argyll-Robertson pupils (small pupils with light-near dissociation) to neurosyphilis with pretectal involvement, and separate them from dorsal midbrain syndrome, Horner syndrome and oculomotor palsy.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1044" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-17", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 39-year-old male patient presented with unilateral miosis, lid drooping and apparent enophthalmos. Which of the following might be the cause for that presentation?", options:["Dorsal midbrain syndrome","Argyll-Robertson syndrome","Horner syndrome","Oculomotor nerve palsy"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Unilateral miosis, lid drooping and apparent enophthalmos are three of the signs of Horner syndrome. The signs are miosis, dilation lag, ptosis, apparent enophthalmos, anhydrosis and, when congenital, heterochromia (L22, slide 11). Horner syndrome is a lesion anywhere along the sympathetic pathway (L22, slide 10). The pathway runs from the hypothalamus down the cord to T1, out to the superior cervical ganglion, up along the carotid artery and through the cavernous sinus into the orbit, ending on the dilator pupillae, Müller's muscles and the orbital vessels (L22, slide 10). Loss of the dilator pupillae gives the small pupil, and loss of Müller's muscle, which helps to hold the upper lid up, gives the mild ptosis (not taken from the course material). The eye looks sunken because the palpebral fissure is narrowed and not because the globe has moved back, which is why the enophthalmos is only apparent (not taken from the course material). Dilation lag means the small pupil is slow to dilate in the dark, so the difference between the two pupils is greater in dim light (not taken from the course material).\n\nDorsal midbrain syndrome is wrong because it gives upward gaze paralysis, convergence-retraction nystagmus, lid retraction and light-near dissociation (L22, slide 9). Lid retraction is the opposite of ptosis.\n\nArgyll-Robertson syndrome is wrong because it gives small pupils with light-near dissociation from neurosyphilis (L22, slide 8). It is usually bilateral and comes without ptosis or enophthalmos (not taken from the course material).\n\nOculomotor nerve palsy is wrong because it also causes ptosis, but the pupil is dilated (L22, slide 4), not constricted. The eye is also turned down and out (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise the Horner syndrome triad of miosis, ptosis and apparent enophthalmos as a sympathetic pathway lesion, and separate it from dorsal midbrain syndrome, Argyll-Robertson syndrome and oculomotor nerve palsy.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1046" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-19", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 67-year-old female patient presented with acute unilateral diminished vision, jaw claudication and severe temporal headache. Which of the following is the most likely cause of that patient's condition?", options:["Non-arteritic optic neuropathy","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Optic neuritis","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Acute unilateral loss of vision with severe temporal headache and jaw claudication in a 67-year-old woman is anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, here the arteritic form caused by giant cell arteritis (not taken from the course material). Jaw claudication and temporal headache in an elderly patient point to giant cell arteritis, an inflammation of the temporal and other cranial arteries that occludes the arteries feeding the optic nerve head (not taken from the course material). Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy shows a swollen pale disc that later becomes a pale flat disc, with an altitudinal field defect (L21, slide 9). The case of a 90-year-old woman with vision loss in one eye, a persistent headache, fatigue and weight loss had acuity of 20/80, a right relative afferent pupillary defect, inferior field loss and a swollen disc with flame-shaped haemorrhages, and her diagnosis was anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (L31,32, slides 20 to 23).\n\nNon-arteritic optic neuropathy is wrong here. Non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is the form without arteritis, and it comes without jaw claudication and temporal headache (not taken from the course material). It is itself a subtype of anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, so it overlaps with anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, the answer here.\n\nOptic neuritis is wrong because it gives unilateral loss of vision with a central scotoma, a relative afferent pupillary defect and only mild disc elevation (L21, slide 8). Its case is a 24-year-old woman with papillitis (L31,32, slides 12 to 16). It does not come with jaw claudication or a temporal headache (not taken from the course material).\n\nNone of the above is wrong because the picture fits anterior ischemic optic neuropathy.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise anterior ischemic optic neuropathy from acute unilateral visual loss with jaw claudication and temporal headache in an elderly patient, and separate it from optic neuritis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1050" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-20", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"27-year-old female patient complains of lid drooping coupled with double vision. History revealed generalized weakness and fatigue. Ocular examination showed intact pupils with marked bilateral ptosis affection of ocular motility. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?", options:["Oculomotor nerve palsy","Trochlear nerve palsy","Myasthenia gravis","Dorsal midbrain syndrome"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Ptosis with double vision, generalized weakness and fatigue, and normal pupils in a 27-year-old woman is myasthenia gravis. Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease of the neuromuscular junction in which antibodies attack the acetylcholine receptors, so skeletal muscles tire with use (not taken from the course material). The levator palpebrae and the extraocular muscles are affected early, so ptosis and diplopia are the usual first signs, and they worsen with sustained use of the muscles (not taken from the course material). The pupil is spared because myasthenia affects striated muscle and not the smooth muscle of the iris (not taken from the course material). The generalized weakness and fatigue, and a young female patient, fit the disease (not taken from the course material).\n\nOculomotor nerve palsy is wrong because a third nerve lesion with pupil involvement gives a dilated pupil (L22, slide 4). It also gives ptosis with an eye turned down and out, is usually unilateral, and does not come with generalized weakness (not taken from the course material).\n\nTrochlear nerve palsy is wrong because the fourth nerve supplies only the superior oblique, so it causes vertical or torsional double vision with a head tilt and no ptosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nDorsal midbrain syndrome is wrong because it gives upward gaze paralysis, convergence-retraction nystagmus, lid retraction and light-near dissociation (L22, slide 9). Lid retraction is the opposite of ptosis, and the pupils show light-near dissociation and are not intact.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise myasthenia gravis from fatigable bilateral ptosis and diplopia with generalized weakness and spared pupils, and separate it from oculomotor palsy, trochlear palsy and dorsal midbrain syndrome.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1052" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-21", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"53 years old patient complaining of near vision problems. The patient is asthmatic and has some degree of heart block that is controlled medically. His visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes. While examination you notice nuclear sclerosis in both eyes. IOP is 28 mmHg in his right eye and 30 mmHg in his left eye, the fundus examination revealed large cup/disc ratio in both eyes. You suspect that patient has POAG. What further tests will help you to confirm the diagnosis?", options:["Visual field test","Fluoresceine angiography","Amsler grid test","OCT for macula"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Primary open-angle glaucoma is defined by a triad: elevated intraocular pressure, optic nerve affection (cupping) and visual field defects (L11). This man already shows two of the three. Pressures of 28 and 30 mmHg are above the usual upper limit of 21 mmHg (not taken from the course material), and the large cup/disc ratio in both eyes is the sign of neural rim thinning, where a cup/disc ratio above 0.3 is the clinical marker (L11). The missing component is the field defect, so a visual field test is the confirming step. Glaucomatous field loss appears as paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum's area (10 to 20 degrees of the central field), arcuate scotomata, nasal step, ring scotoma, constriction of the field and a temporal island of vision (L11). Detecting glaucoma in the early asymptomatic stage usually needs visual field testing, OCT and optic nerve photography (L35,36).\n\nHis 6/6 acuity does not argue against the diagnosis, because the disease is most commonly asymptomatic and central vision is lost only in end-stage disease (L11). A near-vision complaint at 53 is also expected from presbyopia (not taken from the course material), although frequent change of reading glasses is a recognised, less common presentation of the disease (L11). Nuclear sclerosis is an age-related lens change and does not explain raised pressure or disc cupping (not taken from the course material). Asthma and heart block matter for treatment, not for the test: beta-blockers lower aqueous production (L11) but are usually avoided in such patients (not taken from the course material).\n\nFluorescein angiography images the retinal circulation and gives no information about the visual field or the optic nerve rim (not taken from the course material). The Amsler grid tests only the central 10° of the visual field (L1,2, slide 123), while glaucomatous scotomata begin in Bjerrum's area at 10 to 20 degrees and extend to arcuate, nasal step and peripheral defects (L11), so the grid cannot show them. OCT for the macula images the fovea (not taken from the course material), where function is normal here; glaucomatous damage sits at the optic nerve head and retinal nerve fibre layer (L35,36), so a macular scan does not supply the missing field defect.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Visual field testing detects the characteristic arcuate scotomas of glaucoma. It confirms functional loss from optic nerve damage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Confirm suspected primary open-angle glaucoma by completing the triad of raised intraocular pressure, optic nerve cupping and a visual field defect, which needs visual field testing.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1054" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-22", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 64-year-old man presented for evaluation of binocular horizontal diplopia that began 2 weeks earlier and was most bothersome when viewing at a distance. He noted that the diplopia was more prominent when he directed his gaze to the left but otherwise it was present “all the time.” He had a history of hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and dyslipidemia. On examination, of the patient’s left eye showed esodeviation and did not fully abduct, the fundus examination was normal. What is your diagnosis?", options:["Myasthenia gravis","6th nerve palsy","3rd nerve palsy","Increase ICP"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"A left eye that is esotropic in the primary position, adducts fully and fails to abduct is the pattern of sixth nerve palsy (L30, slide 15). The stem gives exactly this: esodeviation of the left eye, incomplete abduction, and horizontal diplopia. The lateral rectus abducts the eye and the sixth nerve supplies it (not taken from the course material), so the deficit shows most when the gaze goes to the left, which is when the diplopia worsens. Diplopia is more bothersome at distance because the esodeviation of a sixth nerve palsy is greater when looking far (not taken from the course material). A 64-year-old with hypertension, diabetes mellitus and dyslipidemia is the usual setting for an isolated microvascular sixth nerve palsy (not taken from the course material), and the normal fundus fits an isolated nerve lesion.\n\nMyasthenia gravis can imitate any pattern of nerve or muscle palsy (L30, slide 20), so it is the mimic to consider, but its ptosis worsens as upgaze is held (L30, slide 18) and lifts with an ice pack (L30, slide 19). This diplopia is present all the time and no ptosis or fatigability is described, so nothing points to it.\n\nThird nerve palsy gives ptosis, an eye turned down and out, and failure of adduction and elevation, with abduction intact (L30, slide 11), and a compressive lesion also dilates the pupil (L30, slide 13). This patient has the reverse: the left eye is turned in and cannot abduct, and neither ptosis nor a pupil change is described.\n\nIncreased ICP is not the diagnosis. Its disc sign is papilledema, which is bilateral with normal visual acuity (L21, slide 8), and the fundus here is normal. Raised intracranial pressure can cause a sixth nerve palsy as a false-localising sign (not taken from the course material), but without disc swelling nothing in this stem points to it, and the isolated horizontal deficit with a normal fundus is explained by the nerve palsy itself.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Sixth nerve palsy causes horizontal diplopia and limited abduction due to lateral rectus weakness. It is often related to diabetes or hypertension.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise sixth nerve palsy from esotropia in the primary position, full adduction and deficient abduction with horizontal diplopia, and separate it from myasthenia gravis, third nerve palsy and raised intracranial pressure.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1056" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-23", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"45-year-old woman comes to the emergency center because of severe left sided headache and double vision that began the night before, immediately following vigorous exercise. As you examine her, she continues to complain of severe left-sided headache. Her neuro-ophthalmologic examination is normal, except that the left upper eyelid is ptotic; and the left pupil is dilated 3 mm more than the right and responds very poorly to light. Her motility looks grossly normal.What is your diagnosis?", options:["Myasthenia gravis","Optic nerve disease","Third nerve palsy","Diabetic neuropathy"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Ptosis with a dilated pupil that reacts very poorly to light is a third nerve palsy with pupil involvement. Third nerve paralysis is a parasympathetic paralysis and causes mydriasis (L22, slide 4), and a complete palsy adds ptosis, an eye turned down and out, and failure of adduction and elevation (L30, slides 11 and 12). Here the left lid is ptotic and the left pupil is 3 mm larger than the right.\n\nThe pupil also points to the cause. In a pupil-involved palsy the usual lesion is compression: an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery presses on the pupillary fibres, which run around the outer rim of the nerve, and the pupil dilates (L30, slide 13). Vascular disease of the vasa vasorum instead infarcts the central fibres and spares the pupil (L30, slide 13). The severe headache beginning right after vigorous exercise fits an aneurysm rather than an ischaemic palsy (not taken from the course material). The motility is described as grossly normal, so this is an early or partial palsy and not the complete picture above (not taken from the course material); the diagnosis rests on the ptosis and the dilated, poorly reactive pupil.\n\nMyasthenia gravis gives ptosis that worsens as upgaze is held (L30, slide 18) and lifts with an ice pack (L30, slide 19). It spares the pupil (not taken from the course material), so a dilated, poorly reactive pupil with severe headache does not fit.\n\nOptic nerve disease lowers visual acuity, colour vision and contrast sensitivity and causes a field defect and a relative afferent pupillary defect (L21, slide 3). None of these is described in a neuro-ophthalmologic examination that is normal apart from the lid and pupil, and optic nerve disease does not cause ptosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nDiabetic neuropathy is the usual cause of the vascular type of third nerve palsy (not taken from the course material), and that type spares the pupil (L30, slide 13). This pupil is dilated and poorly reactive, so the pattern is the opposite, and a diabetic palsy would not explain the abrupt severe headache (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Ptosis with a dilated, poorly reactive pupil indicates parasympathetic involvement of the oculomotor (III) nerve. The normal motility suggests partial or early third nerve palsy, often due to aneurysm or vascular compression.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise third nerve palsy from ptosis with a dilated, poorly reactive pupil, and link pupil involvement to compression, for example by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm, while pupil sparing goes with vascular disease.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1058" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-24", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 34-year-old man presented to his family doctor complaining of some visual distortion, he had noticed in the past week intermittent period of double vision and mild ptosis, and then things went back to normal. He reported some difficulties in lifting things with his arm. He did not report any other neurologic symptoms. Ocular examination was normal except for mild ptosis appear with prolonged upgaze and improve with ice pack. What is the proper management for this case?", options:["Assurance and follow up of the patient","CT orbit and lab investigation in form of T3, T4, and TSH","Topical steroid eye drops and follow up in one week.","Make an appointment with ophthalmology and neurology clinic"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Ptosis that appears with prolonged upgaze and improves with an ice pack, with intermittent diplopia, is the picture of myasthenia gravis. The ptosis worsens as upgaze is held (L30, slide 18) and lifts after an ice pack (L30, slide 19). Antibodies block the acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction and reduce transmission (L30, slide 17), and myasthenia can imitate any nerve or muscle palsy, which explains the intermittent double vision (L30, slide 20). The difficulty lifting things with the arm points to weakness beyond the eyes, so this may already be generalised disease and not a purely ocular one (not taken from the course material). The investigations are acetylcholine receptor antibodies, EMG and chest imaging for an enlarged thymus (L30, slide 21). Making the diagnosis and treating myasthenia, including any systemic involvement, is the work of a neurology team alongside ophthalmology for the eye signs, so an appointment with both clinics is the proper management (not taken from the course material). Treatment itself, such as cholinesterase inhibitors, immunosuppression or thymectomy, is decided there (not taken from the course material).\n\nAssurance and follow-up is wrong because intermittent symptoms are the fluctuating course of myasthenia and not resolution, and limb weakness needs assessment and not reassurance (not taken from the course material). Waiting risks a delay in diagnosis and treatment, as the disease may spread to other muscles (not taken from the course material).\n\nCT orbit and T3, T4 and TSH suit thyroid eye disease, which shows lid retraction and lid lag (L10, slide 17), proptosis (L10, slide 18) and restrictive myopathy (L10, slide 19). This man has ptosis that fatigues and eases with ice, the opposite of retraction, and thyroid eye disease does not improve with an ice pack (not taken from the course material). His ocular examination is otherwise normal, so there is no orbital lesion to image.\n\nTopical steroid eye drops and follow-up in one week is wrong because there is no ocular surface or intraocular inflammation to treat, the examination being normal apart from the ptosis. Eye drops act on the eye surface and do not reach the neuromuscular junction (not taken from the course material), and a one-week delay would leave a probable systemic disease unassessed.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise fatigable ptosis relieved by an ice pack, with intermittent diplopia and arm weakness, as myasthenia gravis needing referral to ophthalmology and neurology.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1060" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-25", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"45 years old woman presented to her family doctor complaining of diplopia. On examination there was retraction of upper eyelid of right eye, proptosis and limited ocular movement especially in up-gaze, with mild pain. Pupil of the right eye was slightly dilated and showed sluggish reaction to light compared to her left eye. What is your diagnosis?", options:["Myasthenia gravis","Thyroid eye disease","3rd nerve palsy due to microvascular ischemic lesion","3rd nerve palsy due to aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Upper lid retraction, proptosis and limited eye movement worst in up-gaze, with diplopia and mild pain in a 45-year-old woman, make thyroid eye disease. Each feature is part of its clinical picture: lid retraction with the Dalrymple, Kocher and von Graefe lid signs (L10, slide 17), proptosis (L10, slide 18), and restrictive myopathy with double vision and discomfort in some positions of gaze (L10, slide 19). Thyroid eye disease is the example of restrictive myopathy among the causes of ophthalmoplegia (L10, slide 10), and Graves disease is more common in females (L10, slide 15). Limited elevation is the typical restriction because the inferior rectus is tethered (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe slightly dilated, sluggish pupil of the right eye is not part of the classic thyroid eye disease picture (not taken from the course material). Optic nerve involvement in thyroid eye disease is tested by visual acuity, colour desaturation, relative afferent pupillary defect and visual fields (L10, slide 20). The pupil is what makes the two third nerve options tempting, but neither explains the lid retraction and proptosis.\n\nMyasthenia gravis gives ptosis, not lid retraction, and the ptosis worsens as upgaze is held (L30, slide 18) and eases with an ice pack (L30, slide 19). It can imitate any pattern of limited movement (L30, slide 20), but it does not cause proptosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nThird nerve palsy due to a microvascular ischemic lesion infarcts the central fibres from disease of the vasa vasorum and spares the pupil (L30, slide 13), whereas this pupil is slightly dilated and sluggish. A third nerve palsy also gives ptosis with the eye turned down and out (L30, slides 11 and 12), the opposite of lid retraction, and does not produce proptosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nThird nerve palsy due to an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery compresses the pupillary fibres and dilates the pupil (L30, slide 13), with ptosis and an eye turned down and out (L30, slide 12). It does not explain lid retraction or proptosis, and its pain is typically a severe abrupt headache, not mild pain (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Proptosis, lid retraction, and restricted upgaze with mild pain are typical of thyroid eye disease due to autoimmune inflammation of extraocular muscles. The sluggish pupil helps rule out isolated nerve palsy.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise thyroid eye disease from lid retraction, proptosis and restricted up-gaze, and separate it from myasthenia gravis and from third nerve palsy of ischemic or aneurysmal cause.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1062" },

{ id:"ophep-neuro-ophthalmology-26", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A male patient 30 years old with visual acuity of 6/6 in both eyes. Twelve hours ago he presented with drop of vision of the left eye. On examination, visual acuity was 6/6 in the right eye and 6/60 in the left eye. Fundus examination showed blurred edges of the left optic disc. The most probable diagnosis is:", options:["Raised intra cranial pressure","Raised ocular tension","Central retinal artery occlusion","Optic neuritis"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Loss of vision to 6/60 in one eye over 12 hours, with a normal fellow eye at 6/6 and a blurred optic disc, is optic neuritis. The papillitis form, with a swollen disc, is unilateral with reduced visual acuity, colour vision and contrast, a central scotoma and a marked relative afferent pupillary defect (L21, slide 8), and acute optic neuritis gives a central scotoma (L21, slide 4). Its causes include multiple sclerosis, viral and toxic causes (L21, slide 8). A near-identical case appears in L31,32: sudden vision loss in one eye of a 24-year-old woman (L31,32, slide 12), acuity reduced in that eye only, with a relative afferent pupillary defect (L31,32, slide 13), a swollen optic disc (L31,32, slide 14), and the diagnosis papillitis (L31,32, slide 16). Papillitis is the disc-swelling form of optic neuritis (not taken from the course material).\n\nRaised intracranial pressure is the cause of papilledema, which is bilateral with normal visual acuity, normal colour vision and only transient obscurations, and no relative afferent pupillary defect (L21, slide 8). This patient has unilateral disc blurring with a fall in acuity to 6/60 while the other eye stays at 6/6, so it does not fit.\n\nRaised ocular tension damages the optic nerve by cupping: chronic glaucoma enlarges the cup/disc ratio and thins the neural rim (L11), and primary open-angle glaucoma is most commonly asymptomatic, with central vision lost only late (L11). That is a slow cupped disc, not an acute blurred and swollen one. An acute rise in pressure would give a painful red eye with a hazy cornea (not taken from the course material), which the stem does not describe.\n\nCentral retinal artery occlusion gives sudden profound vision loss (L31,32, slide 4), a relative afferent pupillary defect (L31,32, slide 6) and a white opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot (L31,32, slide 7). Vision falls to worse than 6/60 (L19.2). The finding here is a blurred disc and no retinal whitening or cherry-red spot is described, so it does not fit.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise optic neuritis from acute unilateral loss of acuity with a blurred optic disc, and separate it from papilledema of raised intracranial pressure, glaucoma and central retinal artery occlusion.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1064" },

];
